whytop for Debian
=================

Updates
-------

Upstream whytop checks GitHub for a newer release and offers to replace its
own binary. This package does not: the check is removed at build time by
debian/rules, which sets

    -X github.com/archesterr/whytop/internal/update.BuiltWithoutUpdateCheck=1

so the packaged binary makes no network connection of its own at all.

apt owns updates for this copy. Upstream also detects a packaged binary at
runtime and refuses to replace it, naming the apt command instead, but a
package in the archive should not contain the code path at all — hence both.

Privileges
----------

The package installs /usr/bin/whytop with no set-user-ID bit and no file
capabilities. whytop is useful as an ordinary user; other users' sockets,
disk I/O and open files are visible only with additional privilege.

Granting that privilege is the administrator's decision, not the package's.
Prefer capabilities over running the whole program as root:

    setcap cap_sys_ptrace,cap_dac_read_search,cap_sys_admin+ep /usr/bin/whytop

Note that cap_sys_admin is broad. On a machine where the extra visibility is
not needed, run whytop unprivileged and accept that some columns read "needs
root".

Every privileged action whytop takes — signals, unit restarts,
daemon-reload, emptying a file, closing a descriptor — is logged to syslog
under AUTHPRIV, naming the SUDO_USER responsible. Read-only browsing is not
logged.

Optional dependencies
---------------------

 * systemd (Recommends) — unit attribution, unit status and the journal
   panel. whytop runs without it; those panels say so instead.
 * iproute2 (Suggests) — the per-process network columns use ss(8).
 * gdb (Suggests) — only for the "close a descriptor" action, which has no
   syscall and is implemented by attaching to the target. Every other
   action works without gdb.

